1
00:00:00,206 --> 00:00:07,748
All right, let's just dive straight into this explainer because today we're looking at some absolutely fascinating new research out of Tsinghua University.

2
00:00:07,950 --> 00:00:12,480
And honestly, it proposes a totally new way to scale artificial general intelligence.

3
00:00:12,942 --> 00:00:18,180
Look, if you've been watching the massive investments pouring into AI lately, you know the current game plan, right?

4
00:00:18,414 --> 00:00:23,332
It's a relentless race for more data, more parameters, just more hardware.

5
00:00:23,694 --> 00:00:27,908
But what if that whole brute force strategy is actually about to hit a massive brick wall?

6
00:00:28,878 --> 00:00:34,800
To really answer that, we've got to understand this huge roadblock that AI developers globally are currently staring down.

7
00:00:35,246 --> 00:00:37,540
We've kind of been operating under this big assumption.

8
00:00:37,774 --> 00:00:43,360
Just keep feeding the machine more data, plug in more GPUs, and boom, we'll eventually hit AGI.

9
00:00:43,726 --> 00:00:50,500
But actually, that assumption has a fatal flaw, which the researchers have creatively dubbed the interaction problem at scale.

10
00:00:51,246 --> 00:00:52,452
So let's unpack that.

11
00:00:53,070 --> 00:00:55,588
Section one, the AI scaling bottleneck.

12
00:00:56,238 --> 00:01:05,840
Right now, we're basically trying to climb this incredibly steep mountain toward AGI by just throwing massive, and I mean massive amounts of data and computing power at the problem.

13
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:09,764
But here's the thing: this brute force approach, it's failing.

14
00:01:10,158 --> 00:01:15,012
It's getting wildly expensive, and the return on investment we get for every new interaction is shrinking.

15
00:01:15,662 --> 00:01:19,600
We're essentially stuck halfway up this mountain in what the researchers call era two.

16
00:01:20,366 --> 00:01:24,612
Now, era one, that was all about scaling model capacity up until roughly 2020.

17
00:01:25,102 --> 00:01:27,748
Era two has been all about scaling data and compute.

18
00:01:28,462 --> 00:01:31,748
But right ahead of us, we're slamming into an accumulation frontier.

19
00:01:32,142 --> 00:01:35,300
We're actually running out of high-quality, human-generated data.

20
00:01:35,790 --> 00:01:40,292
I mean, the entire stock of public text on the internet might literally be depleted within this decade.

21
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:41,520
Crazy, right?

22
00:01:41,806 --> 00:01:44,484
Plus, physically, we are maxing out our computing limits.

23
00:01:44,942 --> 00:01:48,228
Put simply, we just can't keep scaling unconditionally anymore.

24
00:01:49,038 --> 00:01:55,972
So to keep moving up that mountain, we have to totally shift gears from just blindly scaling data and compute to a completely new paradigm.

25
00:01:56,366 --> 00:01:58,080
Welcome to Era 3.

26
00:01:58,606 --> 00:02:03,200
The researchers suggest the next big leap won't actually come from just making our main AI bigger.

27
00:02:03,502 --> 00:02:08,240
Instead, it's going to come from co-designing two separate AI systems to manage resources efficiently.

28
00:02:08,494 --> 00:02:12,548
Basically, one system to make the smart decisions and another to do the heavy lifting.

29
00:02:13,358 --> 00:02:15,204
The superparrot hypothesis.

30
00:02:15,726 --> 00:02:22,720
This concept is so cool because it tracks AI's evolution from just mimicking us to actually having independent thought.

31
00:02:22,894 --> 00:02:26,960
And it explains exactly why this new efficiency is so absolutely critical.

32
00:02:27,406 --> 00:02:36,000
Stage one was your classic stochastic parrot, you know, essentially a highly complex autocomplete that mimics human text without actually understanding a word of it.

33
00:02:36,366 --> 00:02:44,080
Then we hit stage two, which is where we've been recently, using chain of thought and reinforcement learning to develop reasoning that we can still somewhat interpret.

34
00:02:44,430 --> 00:02:47,040
But stage three, that is where things get wild.

35
00:02:47,342 --> 00:02:52,480
AI starts encoding information in self-coded representations that go way beyond human interpretation.

36
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:54,788
It literally begins to self-evolve.

37
00:02:55,438 --> 00:02:57,600
But here's the catch with this timeline.

38
00:02:57,966 --> 00:03:01,252
AI training AI creates a humongous bottleneck.

39
00:03:01,646 --> 00:03:02,160
Think about it.

40
00:03:02,446 --> 00:03:14,960
When the system generates its own data, say it's exploring a robotic simulation or generating a bunch of synthetic reasoning steps, how does it actually internally judge which of those self-generated interactions are worth the expensive compute to execute?

41
00:03:15,470 --> 00:03:22,320
If an AI agent just interacts indiscriminately, it's going to blow its entire budget on experiences that teach it absolutely nothing new.

42
00:03:22,702 --> 00:03:24,932
It's like paying to take a class you've already aced.

43
00:03:25,550 --> 00:03:27,876
Meet System 1 and System 2.

44
00:03:28,526 --> 00:03:35,600
So the researchers propose solving this massive bottleneck by completely overhauling the architecture into a two-part partnership.

45
00:03:35,918 --> 00:03:42,340
We basically need to give the main, heavy-duty executive model a partner whose only job is to judge value.

46
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:53,060
System one acts as an inexpensive bouncer that estimates utility, while the massive, costly System 2 is only allowed to step in and execute interactions that the bouncer deems valuable.

47
00:03:53,678 --> 00:03:56,292
Imagine a giant large language model as System 2.

48
00:03:56,654 --> 00:04:00,356
It takes a ton of compute, time, and memory to run a complex prompt, right?

49
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:05,028
Well, System 1 is a tiny, super lightweight model sitting right in front of it.

50
00:04:05,422 --> 00:04:10,640
System 1 looks at a prompt and goes, wait a second, is System 2 actually going to learn anything from this?

51
00:04:10,926 --> 00:04:12,480
Will this change our belief state?

52
00:04:12,878 --> 00:04:18,692
If the answer is no, System 1 rejects it on the spot, before System 2 ever spends a single expensive dime of compute.

53
00:04:19,374 --> 00:04:20,676
But here's the crucial part.

54
00:04:21,006 --> 00:04:29,476
This isn't just some static router that fires off once, you know, like those older basic systems that send easy questions to a small model and hard ones to a big model.

55
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:30,404
Not at all.

56
00:04:30,766 --> 00:04:32,772
This is a dynamic adaptive loop.

57
00:04:33,294 --> 00:04:37,092
System 1 and System 2 are co designed and co scaled together.

58
00:04:37,550 --> 00:04:43,492
So System 2's actual executed outcomes are constantly fed back to recalibrate System 1's judgment.

59
00:04:43,918 --> 00:04:45,124
It's an active feedback loop.

60
00:04:45,582 --> 00:04:54,276
As System 2 gets smarter and masters new tasks, System 1 learns that those specific tasks are no longer informative, and it just stops letting System 2 waste its time on them.

61
00:04:54,990 --> 00:04:56,740
Predict before you pay.

62
00:04:57,422 --> 00:05:03,648
You really need to grasp the actual mechanism of this conditional interaction to see how we stop all this waste.

63
00:05:03,886 --> 00:05:09,796
And the entire philosophy boils down to this one simple, powerful phrase: predict before you pay.

64
00:05:10,208 --> 00:05:16,288
It's all about ensuring we never ever waste precious resources on tokens or rollouts that teach the model zilch.

65
00:05:16,688 --> 00:05:25,088
In reinforcement learning, for example, if you prompt a model and every single response gets the exact same reward, you get zero relative policy gradient signal.

66
00:05:25,248 --> 00:05:32,548
In plain English, the AI learns absolutely nothing from the exchange, but you still had to foot the massive compute bill to generate all those tokens.

67
00:05:33,038 --> 00:05:37,412
Predict before you pay aims to forecast that uselessness before you run the meter.

68
00:05:38,126 --> 00:05:39,716
Here's how it actually works in practice.

69
00:05:40,142 --> 00:05:42,852
System one scores every single candidate interaction.

70
00:05:43,088 --> 00:05:50,820
If that predicted score doesn't clear a specific threshold, which the researchers represent with the Greek letter kappa, the interaction is just suppressed.

71
00:05:51,022 --> 00:05:52,996
And suppressing it costs almost nothing.

72
00:05:53,198 --> 00:05:56,608
Literally, just the tiny cost of system one making a quick prediction.

73
00:05:56,942 --> 00:06:00,708
That means the massive execution fees of system two are completely saved.

74
00:06:01,358 --> 00:06:04,260
Now, you might be wondering, well, what if system one is wrong?

75
00:06:04,590 --> 00:06:05,328
Great question.

76
00:06:05,582 --> 00:06:08,228
The system intentionally runs what's called an audit sample.

77
00:06:08,462 --> 00:06:14,820
It occasionally executes suppressed items anyway, just to double check its blind spots and make sure system one isn't making bad calls.

78
00:06:15,534 --> 00:06:19,940
So all these clever mechanics build into an overarching four-part architecture.

79
00:06:20,208 --> 00:06:23,088
Represent, predict, gate, and learn.

80
00:06:23,438 --> 00:06:27,972
This is the exact co-design loop that could completely redefine how we train AI.

81
00:06:28,622 --> 00:06:30,084
Let's break down those steps real quick.

82
00:06:30,606 --> 00:06:33,700
Step one, you represent the candidate by embedding it.

83
00:06:34,158 --> 00:06:39,428
Step two, system one cheaply predicts its value, and importantly, its uncertainty.

84
00:06:39,888 --> 00:06:41,488
Step three, you gate it.

85
00:06:41,838 --> 00:06:46,852
A threshold controller dynamically tweaks the admission rate so you always stay perfectly within your compute budget.

86
00:06:47,248 --> 00:06:51,940
And finally, step four, if it's admitted, system two executes and learns.

87
00:06:52,608 --> 00:07:03,140
By constantly embedding, predicting, gating, and feeding those executed outcomes right back into the loop, the AI mathematically minimizes ways to compute while totally maximizing its learning signal.

88
00:07:03,886 --> 00:07:07,588
All right, let's wrap this up with section five, the future of AGI.

89
00:07:08,334 --> 00:07:16,772
This mechanism is a really big deal because it doesn't just save money, it fundamentally redefines how we should even measure AI scaling from here on out.

90
00:07:17,422 --> 00:07:21,700
The old scaling laws, they relied on raw parameters, raw data, raw compute.

91
00:07:22,062 --> 00:07:26,052
But those laws were built on undifferentiated, unconditional interactions.

92
00:07:26,446 --> 00:07:31,780
By suppressing the uninformative garbage data, developers unlock these incredible structural advantages.

93
00:07:32,078 --> 00:07:34,048
They achieve massive screening efficiency.

94
00:07:34,222 --> 00:07:41,168
They drastically reduce peak rollout memory, because, hey, you don't need a giant memory cache for interactions you just predicted are useless, right?

95
00:07:41,486 --> 00:07:44,772
And at deployment, this massively improves test time latency.

96
00:07:45,102 --> 00:07:51,300
You're basically letting the system do way more with a fraction of the hardware, all because it's only paying attention to what actually matters.

97
00:07:52,014 --> 00:07:55,076
The end result here is a completely shifted Pareto Frontier.

98
00:07:55,470 --> 00:07:59,008
Basically, the optimal trade-off point for developers has changed entirely.

99
00:07:59,374 --> 00:08:08,448
Instead of just standard unconditional scaling, these co-designed systems achieve much higher task success rates at significantly lower costs and latency.

100
00:08:08,878 --> 00:08:14,916
You can either get the exact same success for way cheaper, or take your existing budget and achieve vastly more capability.

101
00:08:15,342 --> 00:08:16,004
It's a win-win.

102
00:08:16,654 --> 00:08:21,444
So the major takeaway here is that we need a total mindset shift across the AI industry.

103
00:08:21,710 --> 00:08:28,516
We've got to stop measuring progress just by raw total compute, simply counting the number of operations a GPU crunches.

104
00:08:28,688 --> 00:08:30,048
It's just no longer enough.

105
00:08:30,414 --> 00:08:34,468
We have to start focusing purely on the density of utility-relevant interactions.

106
00:08:34,768 --> 00:08:37,808
Informative compute is greater than total compute.

107
00:08:38,048 --> 00:08:44,964
It's no longer about how many interactions a system can manage, it's entirely about which interactions actually justify their costs.

108
00:08:45,614 --> 00:08:48,448
So I want to leave you with this final thought to chew on.

109
00:08:48,686 --> 00:08:57,220
As we stare down the barrel of exhausted public data and maxed out physical hardware limits, our brute force climb up the AGI mountain is clearly stalling.

110
00:08:57,742 --> 00:09:10,500
But if we stop wasting precious resources teaching AI what it already knows, if we build these brilliant dual system architectures that predict before they pay, is this conditional informative compute the final missing piece?

111
00:09:10,894 --> 00:09:15,812
Could this be the key that propels us past the bottleneck and up to true artificial general intelligence?

112
00:09:16,302 --> 00:09:21,764
It is a thrilling possibility, and one that is absolutely going to reshape how we design AI over the next few years.

